servation belongs primarily to the Disability component of an ABCDE assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Respiratory effort","explanation":"This belongs to Breathing."},{"id":"B","text":"Pulse and skin perfusion","explanation":"These belong to Circulation."},{"id":"C","text":"Consciousness or new confusion","explanation":"Disability includes neurological state."},{"id":"D","text":"Airway obstruction","explanation":"This belongs to Airway."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Use focused ABCDE assessment to identify immediate problems.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration--standard--point-d7b1a704bead","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration::point-d7b1a704bead"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which change is a clear warning of clinical deterioration?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"New confusion with a marked BP fall","explanation":"Both are red flags for impaired perfusion or another acute problem."},{"id":"B","text":"An unchanged chronic symptom with stable observations","explanation":"No acute warning change is described."},{"id":"C","text":"Pain improving without new symptoms","explanation":"This does not identify acute deterioration."},{"id":"D","text":"Improved breathing with stable BP","explanation":"This describes improvement rather than the warning pattern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize sudden clinical red flags.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration--standard--point-d1f0fd2ce29c","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration::point-d1f0fd2ce29c"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"After an intervention for sudden deterioration, what establishes whether it has helped?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The fact that the intervention was prescribed","explanation":"A correct order does not establish effectiveness in this patient."},{"id":"B","text":"The patient’s observations from the previous day alone","explanation":"These are not a current post-intervention assessment."},{"id":"C","text":"The completion of a medication chart entry alone","explanation":"Documentation alone does not measure response."},{"id":"D","text":"Repeat relevant clinical observations and compare the response","explanation":"Response must be assessed rather than inferred from treatment being given."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Reassess after interventions.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration--standard--point-8c26ef3a5a44","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration::point-8c26ef3a5a44"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which focused observation belongs to Exposure in the rapid ABCDE assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"New confusion and reduced responsiveness","explanation":"These primarily assess Disability."},{"id":"B","text":"Visible bleeding and an abnormal line site","explanation":"These are visible potential causes assessed under Exposure."},{"id":"C","text":"Respiratory effort and breath sounds","explanation":"These primarily assess Breathing."},{"id":"D","text":"The ability to speak through a clear airway","explanation":"This primarily assesses Airway."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Exposure assessment includes bleeding, lines, edema, and visible causes.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration--standard--point-e10a7beb3dbf","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration::point-e10a7beb3dbf"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":5},{"id":"ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration--advanced","itemId":"ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration","itemType":"lesson","title":"Sudden Deterioration in CCU: What Do You Do First?","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A previously monitored patient is now unresponsive, not breathing normally, and has no definite pulse after a prompt check. Which response has priority?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Activate the arrest response and begin resuscitation","explanation":"Pulseless deterioration requires immediate action."},{"id":"B","text":"Complete a full medication reconciliation first","explanation":"This is secondary to immediate life support."},{"id":"C","text":"Wait for a laboratory explanation before treating the collapse","explanation":"A possible cause does not justify delaying resuscitation."},{"id":"D","text":"Obtain a routine 12-lead before starting the arrest response","explanation":"A diagnostic tracing must not delay resuscitation."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Treat a pulseless unresponsive patient as an arrest.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration--advanced--point-dc16d1d8520d","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration::point-dc16d1d8520d"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A CCU patient has rapidly worsening dyspnea, falling oxygen saturation, and confusion. Which response is most appropriate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Complete routine discharge education before reassessment","explanation":"This delays response to acute instability."},{"id":"B","text":"Treat the monitor reading without checking breathing or circulation","explanation":"Direct patient assessment remains essential."},{"id":"C","text":"Assess ABCs, obtain current observations, support as indicated, and escalate","explanation":"The clinical deterioration requires simultaneous assessment, support, and help."},{"id":"D","text":"Repeat the previous stable observation values in the chart","explanation":"Current findings must be measured and reported."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Prepare support while escalating an evolving respiratory/perfusion problem.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration--advanced--point-abd978ee28ac","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration::point-abd978ee28ac"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"VT appears on telemetry. Which current bedside information immediately determines the major treatment pathway?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The previous ECG axis and R-wave progression","explanation":"Earlier electrical characteristics do not establish current circulation."},{"id":"B","text":"The last troponin value and planned sampling time","explanation":"Biomarkers must not delay current pulse and perfusion assessment."},{"id":"C","text":"The previous antiarrhythmic dose and its routine due time","explanation":"Medication history matters but does not replace the current circulation assessment."},{"id":"D","text":"Pulse presence and clinical perfusion","explanation":"This distinguishes arrest from a stable or unstable pulse-present problem."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"A dangerous monitor pattern needs immediate patient and pulse correlation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration--advanced--point-a5657323b232","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-07-sudden-deterioration::point-a5657323b232"],"answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":3}],"bankVersion":2,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"aha-arrest-2025","title":"AHA adult cardiac arrest algorithm (2025)","url":"https://cpr.heart.org/-/media/CPR-Files/CPR-Guidelines-Files/2025-Algorithms/Algorithm-ACLS-CA-250527.pdf?sc_lang=en"},{"id":"aha-tachycardia-2025","title":"AHA adult tachyarrhythmia with a pulse algorithm (2025)","url":"https://cpr.heart.org/-/media/CPR-Files/CPR-Guidelines-Files/2025-Algorithms/Algorithm-ACLS-Tachycardia-250514.pdf?sc_lang=en"},{"id":"aha-bradycardia-2025","title":"AHA adult bradycardia with a pulse algorithm (2025)","url":"https://cpr.heart.org/-/media/CPR-Files/CPR-Guidelines-Files/2025-Algorithms/Algorithm-ACLS-Bradycardia-250514.pdf?sc_lang=en"}],"sourceFields":{"sections":[{"label":"Core sequence","text":"Patient first → ABC → monitor → identify danger → escalate → reassess."},{"label":"First 30–60 seconds","text":"• A Airway: can the patient talk? Any obstruction?\n• B Breathing: RR, effort, SpO₂, breath sounds\n• C Circulation: pulse, BP, rhythm, skin/perfusion\n• D Disability: consciousness/confusion\n• E Exposure: bleeding, edema, lines, obvious cause"},{"label":"Red flags","text":"• New severe chest pain\n• Sudden dyspnea\n• Falling SpO₂\n• Hypotension or a marked BP fall\n• New confusion/reduced consciousness\n• Very fast or very slow heart rate with symptoms\n• Dangerous arrhythmia\n• Cold/clammy skin or weak pulse\n• No pulse\n• Sudden major bleeding"},{"label":"Practical actions","text":"• ECG + BP + SpO₂ monitoring\n• Ensure IV access\n• Position appropriately\n• Give oxygen when indicated\n• Obtain 12-lead ECG when relevant\n• Prepare emergency equipment/medications as directed\n• Reassess after every intervention"},{"label":"Examples","text":"Monitor shows VT → look at patient and check pulse. Worsening chest pain → vitals + rhythm + 12-lead + escalate; do not wait for biomarkers. Unresponsive patient → check responsiveness, breathing and pulse; if pulseless, start the arrest response."}]},"readingSections":[{"label":"Core sequence","text":"Patient first → ABC → monitor → identify danger → escalate → reassess.","id":"section-0-8ab20f45d7f2635a"},{"label":"First 30–60 seconds","text":"• A Airway: can the patient talk? Any obstruction?\n• B Breathing: RR, effort, SpO₂, breath sounds\n• C Circulation: pulse, BP, rhythm, skin/perfusion\n• D Disability: consciousness/confusion\n• E Exposure: bleeding, edema, lines, obvious cause","id":"section-1-5387d5038b01cc6f"},{"label":"Red flags","text":"• New severe chest pain\n• Sudden dyspnea\n• Falling SpO₂\n• Hypotension or a marked BP fall\n• New confusion/reduced consciousness\n• Very fast or very slow heart rate with symptoms\n• Dangerous arrhythmia\n• Cold/clammy skin or weak pulse\n• No pulse\n• Sudden major bleeding","id":"section-2-175bca570e7d7642"},{"label":"Practical actions","text":"• ECG + BP + SpO₂ monitoring\n• Ensure IV access\n• Position appropriately\n• Give oxygen when indicated\n• Obtain 12-lead ECG when relevant\n• Prepare emergency equipment/medications as directed\n• Reassess after every intervention","id":"section-3-ed7b2ae9983783eb"},{"label":"Examples","text":"Monitor shows VT → look at patient and check pulse. Worsening chest pain → vitals + rhythm + 12-lead + escalate; do not wait for biomarkers. Unresponsive patient → check responsiveness, breathing and pulse; if pulseless, start the arrest response.","id":"section-4-c7b64b8a2cb4f364"}]},{"itemId":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock","title":"Cardiogenic Shock & Poor Perfusion","itemType":"lesson","exams":[{"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--standard","itemId":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock","itemType":"lesson","title":"Cardiogenic Shock & Poor Perfusion","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"stem":"Which mechanism defines cardiogenic shock?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An empty intravascular tank as the primary mechanism","explanation":"That describes hypovolemic shock."},{"id":"B","text":"Pathological vasodilation as the primary mechanism","explanation":"That describes a distributive pattern."},{"id":"C","text":"Mechanical obstruction to flow as the primary mechanism","explanation":"That describes an obstructive pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"Insufficient cardiac pumping to perfuse organs","explanation":"The primary problem is a weak pump and inadequate effective output."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Cardiogenic shock is pump failure with organ hypoperfusion.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--standard--point-5fd178011d01","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock::point-5fd178011d01"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which cluster supports concern about inadequate organ perfusion?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A normal pain score with no other findings","explanation":"Pain severity does not establish adequate organ perfusion."},{"id":"B","text":"Cold skin, weak pulses, confusion, and low urine output","explanation":"These are indicators of hypoperfusion."},{"id":"C","text":"Warm comfortable skin with stable mentation alone","explanation":"This does not establish the low-output cluster."},{"id":"D","text":"Normal alertness with improving urine output","explanation":"This is more consistent with improvement."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize a low-perfusion cluster.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--standard--point-5acab3c6d1db","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock::point-5acab3c6d1db"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which event most directly provides a pump-failure explanation for shock?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A massive pulmonary embolus obstructing forward flow","explanation":"This primarily supports an obstructive mechanism."},{"id":"B","text":"Major gastrointestinal blood loss with reduced circulating volume","explanation":"This primarily supports a hypovolemic mechanism."},{"id":"C","text":"Acute MI with markedly impaired ventricular contraction","explanation":"Myocardial injury can reduce output."},{"id":"D","text":"Severe infection with predominant loss of vascular tone","explanation":"This primarily supports a distributive mechanism."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"MI, severe HF, arrhythmia, and mechanical problems can impair the pump.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--standard--point-daeb54cca273","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock::point-daeb54cca273"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which pair correctly matches a shock type to its mechanism?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Distributive: excessive dilation; obstructive: blocked flow","explanation":"These are the non-pump mechanisms."},{"id":"B","text":"Cardiogenic: empty tank; hypovolemic: blocked flow","explanation":"This reverses the principal definitions."},{"id":"C","text":"Hypovolemic: excessive dilation; cardiogenic: blocked flow","explanation":"This misidentifies both mechanisms."},{"id":"D","text":"Distributive: empty tank; obstructive: weak contraction","explanation":"Those describe different primary mechanisms."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize distributive and obstructive shock mechanisms.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--standard--point-ae850b96ad8d","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock::point-ae850b96ad8d"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":4},{"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--advanced","itemId":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock","itemType":"lesson","title":"Cardiogenic Shock & Poor Perfusion","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A hypotensive patient has crackles and suspected pump failure. What is the main concern with assuming a large fluid bolus is appropriate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Added fluid reliably distinguishes cardiogenic from hypovolemic shock","explanation":"The whole clinical picture must guide the authorized plan."},{"id":"B","text":"Added fluid directly corrects the loss of myocardial contractility","explanation":"Volume is not an inotrope."},{"id":"C","text":"Added filling load may worsen pulmonary edema","explanation":"A failing pump may not handle the extra volume."},{"id":"D","text":"Added fluid consistently lowers cardiac filling pressure","explanation":"This reverses the immediate loading effect."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"A large automatic fluid bolus may worsen pulmonary edema in pump failure.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--advanced--point-976e3458b01e","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock::point-976e3458b01e"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Patient A has hypotension, raised JVP, and crackles after MI. Patient B has hypotension after major fluid loss, flat neck veins, and clear lungs. Which comparison fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A suggests cardiogenic; B suggests hypovolemic shock","explanation":"The congestion and volume-loss contexts support different mechanisms."},{"id":"B","text":"Both are established as distributive solely from low BP","explanation":"Hypotension alone does not identify the mechanism."},{"id":"C","text":"Both are established as obstructive solely from low BP","explanation":"The cases do not provide that common mechanism."},{"id":"D","text":"A suggests hypovolemic; B suggests cardiogenic shock","explanation":"This reverses the principal patterns."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Differentiate pump failure from volume-loss patterns using the full picture.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--advanced--point-a2b5356936cd","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock::point-a2b5356936cd"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"During treatment, BP improves but confusion, cool skin, and oliguria persist. Which conclusion is most defensible?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Oliguria establishes successful decongestion by itself","explanation":"Low output does not establish a beneficial treatment response."},{"id":"B","text":"Cool skin excludes ongoing shock","explanation":"Coolness can be a hypoperfusion sign."},{"id":"C","text":"The BP improvement establishes complete recovery","explanation":"Persistent organ findings contradict that inference."},{"id":"D","text":"Perfusion remains concerning despite a better BP","explanation":"Multiple organ indicators must improve before response is assumed."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"A BP value must be interpreted with organ perfusion.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock--advanced--point-adbe496f1648","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-08-cardiogenic-shock::point-adbe496f1648"],"answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":3}],"bankVersion":2,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"}],"sourceFields":{"sections":[{"label":"Essential meaning","text":"Cardiogenic shock = the heart cannot pump enough blood to perfuse the organs. Think: weak pump → low cardiac output → organ hypoperfusion."},{"label":"Suspect it when","text":"• Hypotension\n• Cold/clammy extremities\n• Weak peripheral pulses\n• Confusion/reduced consciousness\n• Low urine output\n• Tachycardia or significant bradycardia\n• Dyspnea/crackles may coexist"},{"label":"Common CCU causes","text":"• Acute MI\n• Severe heart failure\n• Serious arrhythmia\n• Mechanical complication after MI\n• Severe valve dysfunction"},{"label":"First nursing response","text":"ABC → BP → rhythm → perfusion → escalate. Assess SpO₂/breathing, consciousness, skin, pulses, urine output, and ensure monitoring/IV access."},{"label":"Important mistake","text":"Do not automatically give a large IV fluid bolus. If the pump is failing, excess fluid can worsen pulmonary edema."},{"label":"Cardiogenic vs hypovolemic","text":"Cardiogenic: pump failure; often cold/clammy, weak pulse, possible crackles/pulmonary edema, JVP may be raised. Hypovolemic: empty tank; often cold/clammy, tachycardic, flat neck veins, clear lungs, with bleeding/dehydration/fluid-loss history."},{"label":"Other shock patterns","text":"Distributive shock: vessels are too dilated; septic shock may be warm early. Obstructive shock: flow is blocked, e.g. tension pneumothorax, tamponade, massive PE."},{"label":"Memory","text":"Cardiogenic = weak pump, often wet lungs/full veins. Hypovolemic = empty tank, usually clear lungs/flat veins. Use the whole clinical picture, not one sign."}]},"readingSections":[{"label":"Essential meaning","text":"Cardiogenic shock = the heart cannot pump enough blood to perfuse the organs. Think: weak pump → low cardiac output → organ hypoperfusion.","id":"section-0-66054e0cd72d998e"},{"label":"Suspect it when","text":"• Hypotension\n• Cold/clammy extremities\n• Weak peripheral pulses\n• Confusion/reduced consciousness\n• Low urine output\n• Tachycardia or significant bradycardia\n• Dyspnea/crackles may coexist","id":"section-1-bdd9c7cdad759857"},{"label":"Common CCU causes","text":"• Acute MI\n• Severe heart failure\n• Serious arrhythmia\n• Mechanical complication after MI\n• Severe valve dysfunction","id":"section-2-f40d45e9e623fe08"},{"label":"First nursing response","text":"ABC → BP → rhythm → perfusion → escalate. Assess SpO₂/breathing, consciousness, skin, pulses, urine output, and ensure monitoring/IV access.","id":"section-3-63bf11371a048fe1"},{"label":"Important mistake","text":"Do not automatically give a large IV fluid bolus. If the pump is failing, excess fluid can worsen pulmonary edema.","id":"section-4-82b891c56be94c79"},{"label":"Cardiogenic vs hypovolemic","text":"Cardiogenic: pump failure; often cold/clammy, weak pulse, possible crackles/pulmonary edema, JVP may be raised. Hypovolemic: empty tank; often cold/clammy, tachycardic, flat neck veins, clear lungs, with bleeding/dehydration/fluid-loss history.","id":"section-5-3fe840cd77eaefa3"},{"label":"Other shock patterns","text":"Distributive shock: vessels are too dilated; septic shock may be warm early. Obstructive shock: flow is blocked, e.g. tension pneumothorax, tamponade, massive PE.","id":"section-6-d31341b0cac14dfb"},{"label":"Memory","text":"Cardiogenic = weak pump, often wet lungs/full veins. Hypovolemic = empty tank, usually clear lungs/flat veins. Use the whole clinical picture, not one sign.","id":"section-7-ee24b46940f64013"}]},{"itemId":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine","title":"Norepinephrine vs Dobutamine","itemType":"lesson","exams":[{"id":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--standard","itemId":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine","itemType":"lesson","title":"Norepinephrine vs Dobutamine","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"stem":"Which primary role best describes norepinephrine in this comparison?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Increase vascular tone and support pressure","explanation":"It is primarily a vasopressor."},{"id":"B","text":"Block the gastric proton pump","explanation":"That is an acid-suppression role."},{"id":"C","text":"Reduce platelet aggregation","explanation":"That is an antiplatelet role."},{"id":"D","text":"Increase renal glucose excretion","explanation":"That is an SGLT2-inhibitor role."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Norepinephrine primarily supports vascular tone and pressure.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--standard--point-34cbee159df1","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine::point-34cbee159df1"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which primary role best describes dobutamine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Suppress gastric acid through proton-pump blockade","explanation":"This is a PPI action."},{"id":"B","text":"Reduce cardiac contractility through beta1 blockade","explanation":"This is the opposite adrenergic action."},{"id":"C","text":"Inhibit platelet P2Y12 receptors","explanation":"This is an antiplatelet action."},{"id":"D","text":"Increase cardiac contractility and output","explanation":"It primarily supports the pump."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Dobutamine primarily supports contractility and output.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--standard--point-b220fc88d7df","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine::point-b220fc88d7df"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which group best evaluates the response and safety of these vasoactive infusions?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"INR, tablet count, and diet history alone","explanation":"These do not establish the immediate infusion response."},{"id":"B","text":"Wound length, suture count, and joint range alone","explanation":"These address another clinical domain."},{"id":"C","text":"BP, rhythm, perfusion, and urine output","explanation":"These follow hemodynamic response and potential adverse effects."},{"id":"D","text":"Stool color, bowel frequency, and oral lesions alone","explanation":"These do not assess the principal hemodynamic effects."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Monitor rhythm, pressure, urine output, and peripheral perfusion.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--standard--point-4da335fed1fe","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine::point-4da335fed1fe"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":3},{"id":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--advanced","itemId":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine","itemType":"lesson","title":"Norepinephrine vs Dobutamine","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A patient has both inadequate contractility and dangerously low arterial pressure. Why might the team prescribe both medicines?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"They are identical drugs with different trade names","explanation":"They have distinct pharmacological roles."},{"id":"B","text":"They address different components of the hemodynamic problem","explanation":"Dobutamine supports contractility while norepinephrine supports vascular pressure."},{"id":"C","text":"They both work by inhibiting cardiac beta1 receptors","explanation":"Both are not beta1 antagonists."},{"id":"D","text":"They replace the need to monitor clinical perfusion","explanation":"Vasoactive treatment increases the need for monitoring."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Pressure and pump support may be combined.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--advanced--point-1455df78142f","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine::point-1455df78142f"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A norepinephrine bag is changed to twice the concentration while the required dose is unchanged. What mL/hour change gives the same drug amount?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Half the previous mL/hour","explanation":"Doubling concentration halves the volume needed for the same dose."},{"id":"B","text":"Four times the previous mL/hour","explanation":"This would cause a still larger drug delivery increase."},{"id":"C","text":"Twice the previous mL/hour","explanation":"This would deliver four times the prior drug amount."},{"id":"D","text":"The same mL/hour","explanation":"This would double the delivered drug amount."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"mL/hour depends on the ordered dose and prepared concentration.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--advanced--point-7dcb7151eebf","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine::point-7dcb7151eebf"],"answerKey":"A"},{"point":"Convert a specified infusion dose and concentration into mL/hour.","format":"numeric-entry","stem":"For an authorized training-case order, the required dose is 4 micrograms/min and the ready infusion concentration is 8 micrograms/mL. What pump rate delivers that dose?","answer":30,"unit":"mL/hour","tolerance":0,"explanation":"4 divided by 8 is 0.5 mL/min; multiply by 60 for 30 mL/hour.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--advanced--point-25a0ccf9688f","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine::point-25a0ccf9688f"]},{"stem":"BP improves during peripheral norepinephrine, but the IV site becomes painful, pale, and cool. What is the best interpretation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Local pain is evidence that the prescribed dose is too low","explanation":"The site change is not a valid reason to increase the infusion."},{"id":"B","text":"Local pallor establishes that the entire circulating volume has fallen","explanation":"The local signs require a site-focused assessment."},{"id":"C","text":"A local infusion complication can coexist with systemic pressure benefit","explanation":"Assess and escalate the threatened site promptly."},{"id":"D","text":"The BP improvement establishes local tissue safety","explanation":"Systemic response does not exclude local ischemia/extravasation."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Norepinephrine monitoring includes peripheral IV site ischemia/extravasation signs.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine--advanced--point-9761561bffe3","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-09-norepi-dobutamine::point-9761561bffe3"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":4}],"bankVersion":2,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"}],"sourceFields":{"sections":[{"label":"The easiest way to remember","text":"Norepinephrine = Pressure. Dobutamine = Pump."},{"label":"Norepinephrine","text":"Think: the blood pressure/perfusion is too low. Norepinephrine mainly increases vascular tone and raises blood pressure."},{"label":"Dobutamine","text":"Think: the heart itself is pumping too weakly. Dobutamine mainly increases cardiac contractility and cardiac output."},{"label":"Why both may be used","text":"If a patient has both a weak pump and dangerously low pressure, the team may use dobutamine for pump support and norepinephrine for pressure support."},{"label":"What you monitor","text":"Norepinephrine: BP/MAP, rhythm, perfusion, urine output, IV site. Dobutamine: HR/rhythm, BP, perfusion, urine output."},{"label":"Safety memory","text":"These are titrated IV infusions. Do not memorize one universal mL/hour rate; the rate depends on the ordered dose and prepared concentration."}]},"readingSections":[{"label":"The easiest way to remember","text":"Norepinephrine = Pressure. Dobutamine = Pump.","id":"section-0-a8e55d4e7fd88d08"},{"label":"Norepinephrine","text":"Think: the blood pressure/perfusion is too low. Norepinephrine mainly increases vascular tone and raises blood pressure.","id":"section-1-1e7e96952d112c44"},{"label":"Dobutamine","text":"Think: the heart itself is pumping too weakly. Dobutamine mainly increases cardiac contractility and cardiac output.","id":"section-2-8a4fc41c1ac1592c"},{"label":"Why both may be used","text":"If a patient has both a weak pump and dangerously low pressure, the team may use dobutamine for pump support and norepinephrine for pressure support.","id":"section-3-c3c6686fc5c8c275"},{"label":"What you monitor","text":"Norepinephrine: BP/MAP, rhythm, perfusion, urine output, IV site. Dobutamine: HR/rhythm, BP, perfusion, urine output.","id":"section-4-717264f9faa89002"},{"label":"Safety memory","text":"These are titrated IV infusions. Do not memorize one universal mL/hour rate; the rate depends on the ordered dose and prepared concentration.","id":"section-5-837952a31ca20eb0"}]},{"itemId":"ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes","title":"Electrolytes That Can Cause Arrhythmias","itemType":"lesson","exams":[{"id":"ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes--standard","itemId":"ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes","itemType":"lesson","title":"Electrolytes That Can Cause Arrhythmias","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"stem":"Which ECG pattern is compatible with low potassium?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A shortened QT as the main calcium pattern","explanation":"This relates to a different electrolyte effect."},{"id":"B","text":"A pacing spike followed by a captured QRS","explanation":"This describes pacing rather than potassium status."},{"id":"C","text":"Independent atrial and ventricular rates alone","explanation":"This identifies a conduction relationship, not a specific low-potassium pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"Flattened T waves or U waves","explanation":"These can accompany hypokalemia and ectopy."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Low potassium can cause flat T waves/U waves and arrhythmia.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes--standard--point-c56cc2792003","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes::point-c56cc2792003"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Why is magnesium relevant when hypokalemia is difficult to correct?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Normal magnesium eliminates every arrhythmia cause","explanation":"Rhythm problems have multiple possible causes."},{"id":"B","text":"Low magnesium can make potassium correction harder","explanation":"These findings have a clinically important relationship."},{"id":"C","text":"Low magnesium reliably shortens the potassium treatment course","explanation":"Deficiency can interfere with correction rather than reliably shorten it."},{"id":"D","text":"Magnesium directly replaces potassium ion for ion","explanation":"They are different electrolytes."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Low magnesium can contribute to ventricular arrhythmia and refractory hypokalemia.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes--standard--point-73070375f78e","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes::point-73070375f78e"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which pairing correctly relates calcium level to the QT interval?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Low calcium: longer QT; high calcium: shorter QT","explanation":"The direction of the QT effect differs with calcium status."},{"id":"B","text":"Low calcium: shorter QT; high calcium: longer QT","explanation":"This reverses the relationship."},{"id":"C","text":"Low calcium: pacing capture; high calcium: AV dissociation","explanation":"Those are not calcium/QT definitions."},{"id":"D","text":"Low calcium: no QT effect; high calcium: no QT effect","explanation":"Calcium influences ventricular repolarization."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Calcium affects QT in opposite directions when low or high.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes--standard--point-519cd17d1574","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes::point-519cd17d1574"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which method of potassium administration is unsafe?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An authorized oral dose when indicated","explanation":"Oral treatment is not the rapid-IV-push hazard."},{"id":"B","text":"A verified protocol-controlled infusion","explanation":"Properly prescribed controlled infusion is a different method."},{"id":"C","text":"Rapid IV push","explanation":"Concentrated rapid IV potassium can cause fatal cardiac effects."},{"id":"D","text":"A prescribed and monitored replacement plan","explanation":"The prohibition concerns uncontrolled rapid IV administration."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Never give IV potassium as a rapid IV push.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes--standard--point-4987f7b0c9f3","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes::point-4987f7b0c9f3"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":4},{"id":"ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes--advanced","itemId":"ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes","itemType":"lesson","title":"Electrolytes That Can Cause Arrhythmias","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A patient with high potassium develops progressive QRS widening and bradycardia. What does the ECG change indicate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An isolated artifact established by the potassium result","explanation":"A known electrolyte problem does not make the ECG change artifact."},{"id":"B","text":"Potentially dangerous electrical toxicity needing urgent escalation","explanation":"Hyperkalemia with ECG changes is a time-critical problem."},{"id":"C","text":"A reliable sign of improved potassium clearance","explanation":"Worsening conduction is not evidence of successful correction."},{"id":"D","text":"An expected therapeutic pacing response","explanation":"No pacing intervention is described."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Severe high potassium can widen QRS and produce bradyarrhythmia/arrest.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes--advanced--point-d6a2ae26a89b","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes::point-d6a2ae26a89b"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A patient receiving a loop diuretic develops new ventricular ectopy. Which investigation pair best checks for a medication-related electrolyte cause?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Amylase and lipase","explanation":"These assess a different organ system."},{"id":"B","text":"Albumin and bilirubin","explanation":"These do not best assess the immediate electrolyte-linked rhythm issue."},{"id":"C","text":"TSH and thyroxine","explanation":"Thyroid testing does not best target the acute loop-diuretic loss context."},{"id":"D","text":"Potassium and magnesium","explanation":"Diuretic-associated losses can contribute to ventricular irritability."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Loop-diuretic-associated PVCs warrant potassium and magnesium review.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes--advanced--point-1870e944c5c0","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes::point-1870e944c5c0"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient has recurrent torsades de pointes. Which electrolyte is specifically important in its treatment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Magnesium","explanation":"Magnesium is important in the treatment of torsades."},{"id":"B","text":"Sodium","explanation":"This is not the specifically highlighted torsades electrolyte."},{"id":"C","text":"Phosphate","explanation":"This is not the specifically highlighted torsades electrolyte."},{"id":"D","text":"Chloride","explanation":"This is not the specifically highlighted torsades electrolyte."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Magnesium is important in torsades de pointes.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes--advanced--point-b18d29833b3d","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-10-electrolytes::point-b18d29833b3d"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":3}],"bankVersion":2,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"}],"sourceFields":{"sections":[{"label":"Potassium K⁺","text":"Potassium strongly affects cardiac electrical activity. Low K⁺ can cause ectopy/arrhythmias and may show flat T waves or U waves. High K⁺ may show tall peaked T waves and, when severe, widening QRS/bradyarrhythmia/arrest."},{"label":"Magnesium Mg²⁺","text":"Low Mg²⁺ makes the heart electrically irritable and can contribute to ventricular arrhythmias. It can also make hypokalemia harder to correct. Magnesium is especially important in torsades de pointes."},{"label":"Calcium Ca²⁺","text":"Calcium affects contraction and the QT interval. Low Ca²⁺ tends to prolong QT; high Ca²⁺ tends to shorten QT."},{"label":"Practical CCU example","text":"A patient on a loop diuretic develops frequent PVCs. Do not only think 'the rhythm changed'—also check K⁺ and Mg²⁺."},{"label":"Nursing safety","text":"If an electrolyte abnormality is accompanied by rhythm/ECG changes, monitor continuously and escalate promptly. Never give IV potassium as a rapid IV push."},{"label":"Memory","text":"K⁺ = rhythm priority. Mg²⁺ = rhythm stability. Ca²⁺ = contraction + QT."}]},"readingSections":[{"label":"Potassium K⁺","text":"Potassium strongly affects cardiac electrical activity. Low K⁺ can cause ectopy/arrhythmias and may show flat T waves or U waves. High K⁺ may show tall peaked T waves and, when severe, widening QRS/bradyarrhythmia/arrest.","id":"section-0-5f58ce00f1749bd2"},{"label":"Magnesium Mg²⁺","text":"Low Mg²⁺ makes the heart electrically irritable and can contribute to ventricular arrhythmias. It can also make hypokalemia harder to correct. Magnesium is especially important in torsades de pointes.","id":"section-1-b229b07552f9157c"},{"label":"Calcium Ca²⁺","text":"Calcium affects contraction and the QT interval. Low Ca²⁺ tends to prolong QT; high Ca²⁺ tends to shorten QT.","id":"section-2-3dd7ba6c7f4333c8"},{"label":"Practical CCU example","text":"A patient on a loop diuretic develops frequent PVCs. Do not only think 'the rhythm changed'—also check K⁺ and Mg²⁺.","id":"section-3-36faa658a0ddef30"},{"label":"Nursing safety","text":"If an electrolyte abnormality is accompanied by rhythm/ECG changes, monitor continuously and escalate promptly. Never give IV potassium as a rapid IV push.","id":"section-4-b1bde656c0ff4510"},{"label":"Memory","text":"K⁺ = rhythm priority. Mg²⁺ = rhythm stability. Ca²⁺ = contraction + QT.","id":"section-5-51a324b87fc98276"}]},{"itemId":"ccu-lesson-11-furosemide","title":"Furosemide in the CCU","itemType":"lesson","exams":[{"id":"ccu-lesson-11-furosemide--standard","itemId":"ccu-lesson-11-furosemide","itemType":"lesson","title":"Furosemide in the CCU","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"stem":"Which presentation most directly supports the use of furosemide?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An isolated mechanical bowel obstruction","explanation":"This is not the diuretic indication."},{"id":"B","text":"Isolated low glucose requiring immediate replacement","explanation":"Furosemide does not supply glucose."},{"id":"C","text":"An isolated oral fungal infection","explanation":"This requires a different treatment."},{"id":"D","text":"Breathlessness, crackles, and edema from congestion","explanation":"The loop diuretic helps remove excess fluid."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Furosemide treats congestion and fluid overload.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-11-furosemide--standard--point-944d12a733bc","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-11-furosemide::point-944d12a733bc"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which effect links furosemide to reduced congestion?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Increased platelet aggregation","explanation":"This is not the decongestive mechanism."},{"id":"B","text":"Increased gastric acid secretion","explanation":"This does not remove excess fluid."},{"id":"C","text":"Increased salt and water excretion","explanation":"Diuresis reduces excess fluid burden."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduced ventricular pacing threshold","explanation":"This is not the mechanism."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Furosemide is a loop diuretic that increases urine output.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-11-furosemide--standard--point-d2992603b24b","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-11-furosemide::point-d2992603b24b"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which monitoring group is most relevant during furosemide therapy?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Tendon pain, supplement cations, and antibacterial susceptibility alone","explanation":"These describe a different drug-safety domain."},{"id":"B","text":"BP, urine output, potassium, renal function, and fluid status","explanation":"These follow benefit and important adverse effects."},{"id":"C","text":"INR, platelet aggregation, and vitamin-K intake alone","explanation":"These primarily relate to anticoagulant treatment."},{"id":"D","text":"Oral lesions, fungal culture, and mouth-contact time alone","explanation":"These relate to local antifungal therapy."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Monitor BP, fluid status, kidney function, and potassium.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-11-furosemide--standard--point-53d74b9fefee","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-11-furosemide::point-53d74b9fefee"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":3},{"id":"ccu-lesson-11-furosemide--advanced","itemId":"ccu-lesson-11-furosemide","itemType":"lesson","title":"Furosemide in the CCU","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"After furosemide, urine output increases but the patient is more confused and hypotensive. Which conclusion is appropriate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Diuresis alone does not establish a safe beneficial response","explanation":"Perfusion, BP, renal function, and congestion must be reassessed."},{"id":"B","text":"The dose should be repeated solely because urine increased","explanation":"A further dose requires review of the whole response."},{"id":"C","text":"Confusion establishes that congestion has resolved","explanation":"It may reflect impaired perfusion or another problem."},{"id":"D","text":"Higher urine output proves uncomplicated recovery","explanation":"The new hypotension and confusion are concerning."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Evaluate breathing and edema, not urine volume alone.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-11-furosemide--advanced--point-4fdbe499bda3","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-11-furosemide::point-4fdbe499bda3"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A patient develops PVCs after several furosemide doses. Which electrolyte mechanism best explains the concern?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Direct AV dissociation is the intended loop effect","explanation":"The drug is not intended to produce heart block."},{"id":"B","text":"Direct P2Y12 blockade is the defining loop effect","explanation":"Furosemide is not a P2Y12 antiplatelet."},{"id":"C","text":"Increased potassium retention is the defining loop effect","explanation":"Loop diuretics are not potassium-sparing."},{"id":"D","text":"Potassium and magnesium depletion can increase electrical irritability","explanation":"This connects diuresis with rhythm risk."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"New ectopy during diuresis can be electrolyte-related.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-11-furosemide--advanced--point-6c7bc227eccb","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-11-furosemide::point-6c7bc227eccb"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"After furosemide, crackles improve but the patient develops postural dizziness, a BP fall, and rising creatinine. What interpretation best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The creatinine rise establishes that more fluid removal is always needed","explanation":"Renal deterioration requires context and an authorized review."},{"id":"B","text":"Decongestion may coexist with excessive depletion and renal stress","explanation":"Benefit does not exclude treatment-related harm."},{"id":"C","text":"Postural dizziness establishes recurrent pulmonary edema","explanation":"This does not explain the stated pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"Improved crackles establish a safe volume status despite the other findings","explanation":"The hemodynamic and renal changes remain concerning."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Excess diuresis can cause volume depletion, hypotension, and renal deterioration.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-lesson-11-furosemide--advanced--point-d6f3ea811684","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-lesson-11-furosemide::point-d6f3ea811684"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":3}],"bankVersion":2,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"}],"sourceFields":{"sections":[{"label":"Why the patient may receive it","text":"Think fluid overload/congestion, especially in heart failure: breathlessness + crackles + edema."},{"label":"What furosemide does","text":"Furosemide is a loop diuretic. More urine → less excess fluid → less congestion."},{"label":"What improvement may look like","text":"• Easier breathing\n• Less edema\n• Fewer crackles\n• Reduced fluid overload"},{"label":"What the nurse monitors","text":"• BP\n• Urine output\n• Potassium K⁺\n• Renal function\n• Overall fluid status"},{"label":"Main problems to watch for","text":"• Hypotension\n• Excessive volume depletion/dehydration\n• Hypokalemia\n• Worsening renal function"},{"label":"CCU connection","text":"If a patient receiving furosemide develops new PVCs or another rhythm change, think about K⁺ and Mg²⁺ as possible contributors."},{"label":"Memory","text":"Furosemide = remove excess fluid. Watch BP, urine output, kidney function and K⁺."}]},"readingSections":[{"label":"Why the patient may receive it","text":"Think fluid overload/congestion, especially in heart failure: breathlessness + crackles + edema.","id":"section-0-0c013d45de85a62c"},{"label":"What furosemide does","text":"Furosemide is a loop diuretic. More urine → less excess fluid → less congestion.","id":"section-1-2ca5310f55686012"},{"label":"What improvement may look like","text":"• Easier breathing\n• Less edema\n• Fewer crackles\n• Reduced fluid overload","id":"section-2-b8027458d7c0c8cb"},{"label":"What the nurse monitors","text":"• BP\n• Urine output\n• Potassium K⁺\n• Renal function\n• Overall fluid status","id":"section-3-7d91e75657988e67"},{"label":"Main problems to watch for","text":"• Hypotension\n• Excessive volume depletion/dehydration\n• Hypokalemia\n• Worsening renal function","id":"section-4-4e71fe5355cbc3e8"},{"label":"CCU connection","text":"If a patient receiving furosemide develops new PVCs or another rhythm change, think about K⁺ and Mg²⁺ as possible contributors.","id":"section-5-2103f8e1add599e1"},{"label":"Memory","text":"Furosemide = remove excess fluid. Watch BP, urine output, kidney function and K⁺.","id":"section-6-230b7a06b8e1d013"}]},{"itemId":"ccu-quick-heart-failure","title":"Heart Failure — Practical CCU View","itemType":"lesson","exams":[{"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--standard","itemId":"ccu-quick-heart-failure","itemType":"lesson","title":"Heart Failure — Practical CCU View","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"stem":"Which two clinical dimensions are used in a practical assessment of heart failure?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Platelet inhibition and clot dissolution","explanation":"These are drug mechanisms rather than HF assessment dimensions."},{"id":"B","text":"Bowel obstruction and oral candidiasis","explanation":"These are different clinical problems."},{"id":"C","text":"Congestion and poor perfusion","explanation":"Both may be present and require assessment."},{"id":"D","text":"Drug absorption and tablet dissolution","explanation":"These do not describe the bedside HF pattern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Heart failure can involve congestion, inadequate output, or high filling pressures.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--standard--point-f62ef09c8257","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-f62ef09c8257"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which cluster most strongly supports congestion?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Orthopnea, crackles, edema, and weight gain","explanation":"These are the fluid-backup findings."},{"id":"B","text":"Dry mouth, urinary retention, and blurred vision","explanation":"These suggest an antimuscarinic effect."},{"id":"C","text":"Nystagmus, ataxia, and slurred speech","explanation":"These suggest a different drug-toxicity pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"Low urine output, cold skin, and confusion alone","explanation":"These primarily identify poor perfusion."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Identify pulmonary/systemic congestion.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--standard--point-f1e56d0ac47b","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-f1e56d0ac47b"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which HF finding most directly suggests inadequate organ perfusion?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Isolated peripheral edema without other changes","explanation":"Edema primarily suggests congestion."},{"id":"B","text":"Isolated weight gain without other changes","explanation":"Weight gain primarily reflects a volume trend."},{"id":"C","text":"Isolated raised JVP without other changes","explanation":"Raised venous pressure does not alone establish organ perfusion."},{"id":"D","text":"New confusion with cold skin and oliguria","explanation":"This is a low-perfusion cluster."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Identify poor perfusion in HF.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--standard--point-cc9a37d1c728","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-cc9a37d1c728"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which pairing correctly describes the dominant backup patterns?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Left: GI obstruction; right: urinary obstruction","explanation":"These are different organ problems."},{"id":"B","text":"Left: platelet deficiency; right: clotting-factor deficiency","explanation":"These are not HF backup mechanisms."},{"id":"C","text":"Left: pulmonary congestion; right: systemic venous congestion","explanation":"Lung and systemic venous findings are distinguished."},{"id":"D","text":"Left: systemic venous congestion only; right: pulmonary congestion only","explanation":"This reverses the principal patterns."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Left and right ventricular failure produce different backup patterns.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--standard--point-6006909ae774","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-6006909ae774"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"What does left ventricular ejection fraction describe?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The percentage of circulating platelets activated","explanation":"This is unrelated to ventricular ejection."},{"id":"B","text":"The percentage of LV blood ejected with each contraction","explanation":"It is a fractional measure of systolic emptying."},{"id":"C","text":"The percentage of blood oxygenated in the lungs","explanation":"That is not the definition of EF."},{"id":"D","text":"The percentage of heartbeats conducted through the AV node","explanation":"EF does not measure AV conduction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"EF describes the fraction of LV blood ejected, not the whole clinical severity.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--standard--point-1309b452709a","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-1309b452709a"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":5},{"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--advanced","itemId":"ccu-quick-heart-failure","itemType":"lesson","title":"Heart Failure — Practical CCU View","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A patient has a preserved EF but dyspnea and congestion with impaired ventricular filling. Which interpretation fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A preserved EF proves all symptoms are noncardiac","explanation":"Other cardiac assessment remains necessary."},{"id":"B","text":"A preserved EF excludes every HF diagnosis","explanation":"Heart failure can occur with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF)."},{"id":"C","text":"A preserved EF establishes normal filling pressures","explanation":"Filling abnormalities may coexist with preserved EF."},{"id":"D","text":"HFpEF can occur with impaired filling despite preserved EF","explanation":"EF alone does not exclude HF or determine severity."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Preserved EF does not exclude clinically important HF.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--advanced--point-03676bf960fa","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-03676bf960fa"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient has crackles and edema together with cold skin, hypotension, and confusion. Which classification best captures the bedside picture?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Poorly perfused with congestion excluded","explanation":"Crackles and edema indicate a concurrent congestion concern."},{"id":"B","text":"Both congested and poorly perfused","explanation":"The wet and cold patterns can coexist."},{"id":"C","text":"Congested with adequate perfusion established","explanation":"The hypotension and confusion contradict that assurance."},{"id":"D","text":"Neither congested nor poorly perfused","explanation":"Both sets of findings are present."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Congestion and poor perfusion can coexist.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--advanced--point-539c0d1deede","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-539c0d1deede"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"During HF observation, a patient develops sudden severe dyspnea and reduced consciousness. Which assessment has immediate priority?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Airway, breathing, oxygenation, and circulation with urgent help","explanation":"These findings can signal acute cardiopulmonary deterioration."},{"id":"B","text":"The long-term dietary sodium history first","explanation":"This is relevant later but not the first response to acute compromise."},{"id":"C","text":"The planned exercise schedule first","explanation":"Mobilization planning is secondary to acute stabilization."},{"id":"D","text":"The discharge medication supply first","explanation":"This does not address the current emergency."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Acute respiratory or perfusion deterioration in HF needs escalation.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-quick-heart-failure--advanced--point-40564cb2c6cd","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-heart-failure::point-40564cb2c6cd"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":3}],"bankVersion":2,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"}],"sourceFields":{"sections":[{"label":"Essential meaning","text":"Heart failure means the heart cannot pump enough blood effectively or requires abnormally high filling pressures. Clinically, think in two directions: congestion and poor perfusion."},{"label":"Congestion","text":"Dyspnea, orthopnea, crackles, leg edema, raised JVP, and fluid/weight gain."},{"label":"Poor perfusion","text":"Hypotension, cold/clammy skin, weak pulse, confusion, and low urine output."},{"label":"Left vs right","text":"Left-sided HF mainly backs up into the lungs → dyspnea, orthopnea, crackles, pulmonary edema. Right-sided HF mainly backs up into the systemic veins → peripheral edema, raised JVP, abdominal/hepatic congestion."},{"label":"EF","text":"Ejection fraction is the percentage of LV blood ejected with each contraction. HFrEF = reduced EF. HFpEF = preserved EF but impaired filling/stiffness. Do not judge severity from EF alone."},{"label":"Nursing focus","text":"Breathing + BP + rhythm + perfusion + urine output + fluid balance. Escalate sudden severe dyspnea, pulmonary edema, falling BP, altered consciousness, dangerous arrhythmia, very low urine output, or ischemic chest pain."},{"label":"Memory","text":"HF patients may be 'wet' from congestion, 'cold' from poor perfusion, or both."}]},"readingSections":[{"label":"Essential meaning","text":"Heart failure means the heart cannot pump enough blood effectively or requires abnormally high filling pressures. Clinically, think in two directions: congestion and poor perfusion.","id":"section-0-01038e81ba2c45df"},{"label":"Congestion","text":"Dyspnea, orthopnea, crackles, leg edema, raised JVP, and fluid/weight gain.","id":"section-1-0aa306d375023543"},{"label":"Poor perfusion","text":"Hypotension, cold/clammy skin, weak pulse, confusion, and low urine output.","id":"section-2-f02440d3d7faa6e1"},{"label":"Left vs right","text":"Left-sided HF mainly backs up into the lungs → dyspnea, orthopnea, crackles, pulmonary edema. Right-sided HF mainly backs up into the systemic veins → peripheral edema, raised JVP, abdominal/hepatic congestion.","id":"section-3-098c35a450a36fd1"},{"label":"EF","text":"Ejection fraction is the percentage of LV blood ejected with each contraction. HFrEF = reduced EF. HFpEF = preserved EF but impaired filling/stiffness. Do not judge severity from EF alone.","id":"section-4-35937b540387c344"},{"label":"Nursing focus","text":"Breathing + BP + rhythm + perfusion + urine output + fluid balance. Escalate sudden severe dyspnea, pulmonary edema, falling BP, altered consciousness, dangerous arrhythmia, very low urine output, or ischemic chest pain.","id":"section-5-3c16058b74654c22"},{"label":"Memory","text":"HF patients may be 'wet' from congestion, 'cold' from poor perfusion, or both.","id":"section-6-50455106ebb8affc"}]},{"itemId":"ccu-quick-dcm","title":"Dilated Cardiomyopathy (DCM)","itemType":"lesson","exams":[{"id":"ccu-quick-dcm--standard","itemId":"ccu-quick-dcm","itemType":"lesson","title":"Dilated Cardiomyopathy (DCM)","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"stem":"Which structural/functional pattern characterizes the DCM teaching point?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An isolated electrical AV block without a structural finding","explanation":"That does not define DCM."},{"id":"B","text":"Ventricular enlargement with impaired contraction","explanation":"A dilated weak pump can reduce cardiac output."},{"id":"C","text":"Normal ventricular size with mandatory hypercontractility","explanation":"This is not the DCM pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"An isolated pulmonary airway narrowing","explanation":"That describes a different organ mechanism."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"DCM has ventricular dilation and weak systolic contraction.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-dcm--standard--point-70eb3ca4c427","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-dcm::point-70eb3ca4c427"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which presentation is compatible with a dilated weak ventricle?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Only a painful Achilles tendon","explanation":"This points to another clinical issue."},{"id":"B","text":"Only oral white plaques","explanation":"This is not a characteristic cardiac presentation."},{"id":"C","text":"Dyspnea, fatigue, congestion, and rhythm problems","explanation":"These are relevant HF/arrhythmia manifestations."},{"id":"D","text":"Only dry mouth with urinary retention","explanation":"This fits a different adverse-effect pattern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"DCM can present with HF and arrhythmia.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-dcm--standard--point-d4870f76f59b","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-dcm::point-d4870f76f59b"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which history is a recognized DCM-related contributor?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Myocarditis or a familial cardiomyopathy history","explanation":"These are recognized contributors."},{"id":"B","text":"A remote healed superficial abrasion","explanation":"This does not establish myocardial injury."},{"id":"C","text":"A single normal ECG recording","explanation":"A normal recording is not an etiology."},{"id":"D","text":"An uncomplicated local fungal mouth infection","explanation":"This is not the named myocardial cause."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Myocarditis, inherited disease, or cardiotoxic exposures can contribute to a DCM phenotype.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-dcm--standard--point-7f76481a9a66","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-dcm::point-7f76481a9a66"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which set contains recognized contributors to dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Pin-site irritation, fracture swelling, cast tightness","explanation":"These are limb-related concerns rather than the DCM contributor group."},{"id":"B","text":"Peptic ulcer, oral candidiasis, iron chelation","explanation":"These are not the listed DCM contributor group."},{"id":"C","text":"Peripheral neuropathy, tendon rupture, dry mouth","explanation":"These are adverse-effect patterns of other treatments."},{"id":"D","text":"Familial disease, myocarditis, cardiotoxic exposure","explanation":"All are listed contributors."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize familial, inflammatory, toxic, peripartum, and tachyarrhythmic contributors.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-dcm--standard--point-0515bc625040","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-dcm::point-0515bc625040"],"answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":4},{"id":"ccu-quick-dcm--advanced","itemId":"ccu-quick-dcm","itemType":"lesson","title":"Dilated Cardiomyopathy (DCM)","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A DCM patient develops low urine output and confusion with cool skin. Which new problem is most concerning?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Low-output organ hypoperfusion","explanation":"The findings suggest inadequate effective pumping."},{"id":"B","text":"A therapeutic increase in renal clearance","explanation":"Oliguria does not establish improved clearance."},{"id":"C","text":"A local IV-site problem as the established cause","explanation":"No local site findings explain the systemic pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"An isolated benign change in ventricular size","explanation":"The patient has acute clinical perfusion findings."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Track the clinical weak-pump consequences.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-dcm--advanced--point-f69a5cd7b9d9","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-dcm::point-f69a5cd7b9d9"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A DCM patient’s edema improves, but a new symptomatic arrhythmia appears. Which conclusion best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The arrhythmia proves that the initial DCM diagnosis was impossible","explanation":"Arrhythmias can occur with DCM."},{"id":"B","text":"Improved congestion does not establish rhythm safety","explanation":"Different consequences of the weak-pump condition need separate reassessment."},{"id":"C","text":"Improved edema excludes an arrhythmia complication","explanation":"A better volume sign does not exclude electrical deterioration."},{"id":"D","text":"The rhythm can be ignored until edema returns","explanation":"Symptoms and a new arrhythmia require current assessment."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Monitor rhythm and perfusion as well as congestion.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-dcm--advanced--point-86cf0c72ac28","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-dcm::point-86cf0c72ac28"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":2}],"bankVersion":2,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"esc-cardiomyopathy-2023","title":"ESC cardiomyopathy guideline (2023)","url":"https://academic.oup.com/eurheartj/article/44/37/3503/7246608"}],"sourceFields":{"sections":[{"label":"What it means","text":"DCM = dilated cardiomyopathy. The ventricles, especially the LV, become enlarged and contract weakly → cardiac output falls."},{"label":"What it causes","text":"Often presents as heart failure: dyspnea, orthopnea, fatigue, edema, crackles if congested, and arrhythmias. Severe cases can develop low-output states or cardiogenic shock."},{"label":"Common causes","text":"• Genetic/familial disease\n• Myocarditis\n• Alcohol or cardiotoxic drugs\n• Ischemic heart disease/previous MI\n• Peripartum cardiomyopathy\n• Long-standing tachyarrhythmias\n• Some endocrine, metabolic, or nutritional disorders\n• Sometimes no clear cause is found"},{"label":"Nursing focus","text":"Monitor BP, rhythm, SpO₂, breathing, edema, urine output, and perfusion."},{"label":"Memory","text":"DCM = dilated heart + weak pump → heart failure risk."}]},"readingSections":[{"label":"What it means","text":"DCM = dilated cardiomyopathy. The ventricles, especially the LV, become enlarged and contract weakly → cardiac output falls.","id":"section-0-bcde5f157aabac2c"},{"label":"What it causes","text":"Often presents as heart failure: dyspnea, orthopnea, fatigue, edema, crackles if congested, and arrhythmias. Severe cases can develop low-output states or cardiogenic shock.","id":"section-1-8b0c5b4299bc83a6"},{"label":"Common causes","text":"• Genetic/familial disease\n• Myocarditis\n• Alcohol or cardiotoxic drugs\n• Ischemic heart disease/previous MI\n• Peripartum cardiomyopathy\n• Long-standing tachyarrhythmias\n• Some endocrine, metabolic, or nutritional disorders\n• Sometimes no clear cause is found","id":"section-2-0977c7becc97b273"},{"label":"Nursing focus","text":"Monitor BP, rhythm, SpO₂, breathing, edema, urine output, and perfusion.","id":"section-3-da5a0a719045215d"},{"label":"Memory","text":"DCM = dilated heart + weak pump → heart failure risk.","id":"section-4-81664d27994fefec"}]},{"itemId":"ccu-quick-ihd-mi","title":"Ischemic Heart Disease vs Myocardial Infarction","itemType":"lesson","exams":[{"id":"ccu-quick-ihd-mi--standard","itemId":"ccu-quick-ihd-mi","itemType":"lesson","title":"Ischemic Heart Disease vs Myocardial Infarction","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"stem":"Which description best defines ischemic heart disease?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Only third-degree AV block","explanation":"A conduction diagnosis is not the definition of IHD."},{"id":"B","text":"A broader disease state involving reduced coronary blood supply","explanation":"It includes presentations with and without infarction."},{"id":"C","text":"Only one episode of myocardial cell death","explanation":"That describes MI more specifically."},{"id":"D","text":"Only a normal coronary circulation","explanation":"IHD concerns reduced supply, not its absence."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"IHD is a broad coronary blood-supply disease state.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-ihd-mi--standard--point-5bba3c665169","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-ihd-mi::point-5bba3c665169"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"What makes myocardial infarction more specific than myocardial ischemia alone?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Myocardial cell death","explanation":"Infarction includes necrosis from sufficiently severe or prolonged ischemia."},{"id":"B","text":"Any use of an antiplatelet medicine","explanation":"Treatment does not itself establish the diagnosis."},{"id":"C","text":"Any transient chest discomfort","explanation":"Symptoms alone do not establish cell death."},{"id":"D","text":"Any episode of sinus tachycardia","explanation":"Rate alone does not establish infarction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"MI involves myocardial necrosis from ischemia.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-ihd-mi--standard--point-439565a8d901","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-ihd-mi::point-439565a8d901"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":2},{"id":"ccu-quick-ihd-mi--advanced","itemId":"ccu-quick-ihd-mi","itemType":"lesson","title":"Ischemic Heart Disease vs Myocardial Infarction","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A patient has stable exertional angina without evidence of an infarction event. Which statement is justified?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The absence of MI excludes every ischemic process","explanation":"Ischemia can occur without infarction."},{"id":"B","text":"Stable symptoms establish a noncardiac diagnosis","explanation":"A stable ischemic presentation remains possible."},{"id":"C","text":"The angina label proves myocardial necrosis","explanation":"Angina alone does not establish MI."},{"id":"D","text":"IHD may be present without an MI","explanation":"Ischemic disease is broader than infarction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Not every IHD presentation is an MI.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-ihd-mi--advanced--point-3c6a5b8771cc","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-ihd-mi::point-3c6a5b8771cc"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which relationship between IHD and MI is correct?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"MI excludes reduced coronary supply as its underlying mechanism","explanation":"The MI event is ischemic."},{"id":"B","text":"MI is a myocardial-necrosis event within the broader IHD context","explanation":"Infarction is a specific ischemic event, whereas IHD includes presentations without infarction."},{"id":"C","text":"IHD requires myocardial necrosis in each clinical presentation","explanation":"Ischemia can occur without infarction."},{"id":"D","text":"IHD identifies an electrical rhythm rather than a supply disorder","explanation":"IHD is a coronary blood-supply disease category."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"The relationship is broad disease versus a specific necrotic event.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-quick-ihd-mi--advanced--point-0427c4fd7638","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-quick-ihd-mi::point-0427c4fd7638"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":2}],"bankVersion":2,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"}],"sourceFields":{"sections":[{"label":"IHD","text":"Ischemic heart disease is the broader disease state caused by reduced coronary blood supply. It can present as stable angina, unstable angina, NSTEMI, STEMI, or chronic ischemic cardiomyopathy."},{"label":"MI","text":"Myocardial infarction is a specific event in which ischemia is severe/prolonged enough to cause myocardial cell death."},{"label":"Memory","text":"All MI is ischemic heart disease, but not all ischemic heart disease is MI. A patient with stable exertional angina has IHD but is not necessarily having an MI."}]},"readingSections":[{"label":"IHD","text":"Ischemic heart disease is the broader disease state caused by reduced coronary blood supply. It can present as stable angina, unstable angina, NSTEMI, STEMI, or chronic ischemic cardiomyopathy.","id":"section-0-57d3c8a494ff3891"},{"label":"MI","text":"Myocardial infarction is a specific event in which ischemia is severe/prolonged enough to cause myocardial cell death.","id":"section-1-9018781a4a37352e"},{"label":"Memory","text":"All MI is ischemic heart disease, but not all ischemic heart disease is MI. A patient with stable exertional angina has IHD but is not necessarily having an MI.","id":"section-2-667f3b5366314ed8"}]},{"itemId":"ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety","title":"Fracture & Limb Safety — Neurovascular Check","itemType":"lesson","exams":[{"id":"ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety--standard","itemId":"ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety","itemType":"lesson","title":"Fracture & Limb Safety — Neurovascular Check","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"stem":"Where is the key neurovascular assessment directed after a limb fracture or immobilization?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Only at the opposite limb without checking the injured side","explanation":"Comparison cannot replace assessment of the affected limb."},{"id":"B","text":"Only at the frame’s outer metal surface","explanation":"Hardware appearance does not establish tissue perfusion."},{"id":"C","text":"Only at the uninjured shoulder","explanation":"This does not assess distal tissue at risk."},{"id":"D","text":"Distal to the injury, compared with the other side when possible","explanation":"Tissue below the injury may lose blood flow or nerve function."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Assess distal blood supply and nerve function below an injury/immobilization.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety--standard--point-4361d515fd95","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety::point-4361d515fd95"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which set best represents a focused neurovascular limb assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Pain, blood supply, feeling, movement, and swelling","explanation":"These cover the neurovascular and pressure concerns."},{"id":"B","text":"Temperature and pain score without distal findings","explanation":"These are incomplete for the neurovascular assessment."},{"id":"C","text":"Frame color, screw count, and bandage brand alone","explanation":"Hardware description does not establish distal function."},{"id":"D","text":"Weight, diet, bowel pattern, and sleep alone","explanation":"These do not replace the focused limb assessment."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Pain, circulation, sensation, movement, and swelling form the focused check.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety--standard--point-4cc0e9cbfd91","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety::point-4cc0e9cbfd91"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"What is the central mechanism of acute compartment syndrome?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An isolated superficial skin allergy increases every distal pulse","explanation":"This does not describe compartment physiology."},{"id":"B","text":"A normal distal pulse increases compartment space","explanation":"A pulse does not establish safe compartment pressure."},{"id":"C","text":"Pressure within a closed compartment compromises tissue perfusion","explanation":"Swelling/bleeding can create an ischemia–edema cycle."},{"id":"D","text":"A venous cannula removes too much joint fluid","explanation":"This is not the mechanism responsible."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Raised compartment pressure can reduce perfusion and damage nerve/muscle.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety--standard--point-01c80b666cc2","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety::point-01c80b666cc2"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which new finding is a particularly important early compartment-syndrome warning?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A normal uninjured hand examination","explanation":"This does not establish safety of the affected compartment."},{"id":"B","text":"Comfort improving with unchanged distal findings","explanation":"This does not describe the warning change."},{"id":"C","text":"An unchanged old scar without new symptoms","explanation":"This does not indicate an acute pressure problem."},{"id":"D","text":"Increasing disproportionate pain worsened by passive stretch","explanation":"This is a key early warning."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Disproportionate pain, especially with passive stretch, is an early warning.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety--standard--point-a1ec24eceb4d","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety::point-a1ec24eceb4d"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which task requires a specifically authorized senior/ordered plan rather than independent intern action?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Recognizing a new sensory change","explanation":"Assessment within training is an intern responsibility."},{"id":"B","text":"Documenting the time and response","explanation":"Documentation is a responsibility."},{"id":"C","text":"Altering prescribed immobilization or undertaking an invasive procedure","explanation":"Scope and authorization govern these interventions."},{"id":"D","text":"Reporting a worsening distal examination","explanation":"Prompt reporting is part of the intern role."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Immobilization modification and invasive treatment need authorization.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety--standard--point-213242c60ed6","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety::point-213242c60ed6"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which group represents late ischemic findings that must not be awaited before escalating suspected compartment syndrome?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Increasing pain, pain with stretch, and new tingling","explanation":"These are earlier warning findings rather than signs to await."},{"id":"B","text":"Pallor, paralysis, and absent pulse","explanation":"Waiting for late signs can delay treatment."},{"id":"C","text":"Tense swelling, increasing pain, and new sensory change","explanation":"These already warrant concern."},{"id":"D","text":"Pain out of proportion with a still-palpable pulse","explanation":"This can be an early dangerous presentation."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Pallor, paralysis, and absent pulses are late ischemic findings.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety--standard--point-4e09ce807105","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety::point-4e09ce807105"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":6},{"id":"ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety","itemType":"lesson","title":"Fracture & Limb Safety — Neurovascular Check","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A fracture patient has severe escalating pain, passive-stretch pain, and new tingling, but a distal pulse remains palpable. Which interpretation is safest?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A pulse excludes a significant compartment-pressure problem","explanation":"This is the critical trap identified."},{"id":"B","text":"Tingling proves that every nerve remains unaffected","explanation":"New sensory change increases concern."},{"id":"C","text":"Compartment syndrome remains an emergency concern","explanation":"Pulses can persist while tissue perfusion is threatened."},{"id":"D","text":"Waiting for pulse loss will identify the earliest stage","explanation":"Pulselessness can be a late sign."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"A palpable pulse does not rule out compartment syndrome.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety--advanced--point-1d241a82847f","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety::point-1d241a82847f"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"An intern finds worsening distal sensation and tense swelling in a casted limb. Which action has priority?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Repeat focused findings, protect the limb, and obtain urgent senior assessment","explanation":"Recognition and prompt escalation are within the supervised role."},{"id":"B","text":"Perform the next scheduled dressing inspection before reporting","explanation":"A scheduled task should not delay urgent neurovascular escalation."},{"id":"C","text":"Complete the planned mobility session before reporting","explanation":"Continued movement may be unsafe with deterioration."},{"id":"D","text":"Review the routine analgesic schedule before reporting the new findings","explanation":"Analgesia review must not delay assessment of threatened tissue."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Escalate deterioration within supervised intern scope.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety--advanced--point-7228d4263c1d","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety::point-7228d4263c1d"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A patient’s pain becomes much worse than baseline with new weakness. The next routine observation is not due yet. Which factor determines the timing of reassessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The next routine discharge discussion alone","explanation":"This is secondary to the new limb findings."},{"id":"B","text":"The new clinical change","explanation":"Deterioration warrants reassessment and escalation now."},{"id":"C","text":"The next elective X-ray time alone","explanation":"Imaging timing does not establish the safety of waiting."},{"id":"D","text":"The routine observation time alone","explanation":"A schedule does not override new warning signs."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Do not normalize a new severe pain trend as ordinary fracture pain.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety--advanced--point-177f1bb14f6f","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-01-fracture-limb-safety::point-177f1bb14f6f"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":3}],"bankVersion":2,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"supplied-health-alterations","filename":"05-Health-Alterations-1781195254.pdf"}],"sourceFields":{"sections":[{"label":"The one question","text":"With any injured or immobilized limb, ask: Is the tissue below the injury still getting enough blood and nerve supply? Check distal to the injury/cast/fixator and compare with the opposite side when possible."},{"label":"Memory sequence","text":"PAIN → BLOOD → FEELING → MOVE → SWELLING.\n\n1) PAIN: Is pain suddenly worsening or much worse than expected?\n2) BLOOD: Check color, warmth, capillary refill and distal pulse.\n3) FEELING: Ask about numbness, tingling or burning; compare sensation.\n4) MOVE: Ask the patient to move distal fingers/toes and compare strength/movement.\n5) SWELLING: Look for increasing swelling, a tense limb, or a cast/dressing becoming tight."},{"label":"Compartment syndrome — the emergency","text":"Think: pressure building inside the limb. Trauma/fracture → bleeding and swelling → pressure rises inside a closed muscle compartment → tissue perfusion falls → nerves and muscle can be permanently injured."},{"label":"Early warning to remember","text":"Severe or increasing pain that is out of proportion to the injury, especially pain made worse by passive stretch, is an important warning. A tense swollen compartment and new paresthesia/weakness add concern. Do not wait for every sign to appear."},{"label":"Critical trap","text":"A palpable distal pulse does NOT rule out compartment syndrome. Pallor, paralysis and loss of pulse are late ischemic findings; waiting for the pulse to disappear can delay treatment."},{"label":"Intern responsibilities","text":"Assess distal neurovascular status within your training, recognize deterioration, report findings urgently, document the change and response, and continue reassessment. Check for an obviously constrictive cast/dressing, but do not independently alter prescribed immobilization unless specifically authorized by the local protocol or order."},{"label":"Senior/ordered interventions","text":"Independent treatment decisions, modifying or removing prescribed immobilization/fixation unless specifically authorized, medication changes, compartment-pressure measurement, fasciotomy, and other invasive procedures. Your role is to recognize, report, prepare, and assist within supervision and the authorized MSF/Nasser IBD2 protocol."},{"label":"15-second memory","text":"Fracture patient? Check BELOW the injury: PAIN → BLOOD → FEELING → MOVE → SWELLING. Severe increasing pain + pain with passive stretch = compartment-syndrome warning. A pulse does not rule it out."}]},"readingSections":[{"label":"The one question","text":"With any injured or immobilized limb, ask: Is the tissue below the injury still getting enough blood and nerve supply? Check distal to the injury/cast/fixator and compare with the opposite side when possible.","id":"section-0-a2c5798986110ae0"},{"label":"Memory sequence","text":"PAIN → BLOOD → FEELING → MOVE → SWELLING.\n\n1) PAIN: Is pain suddenly worsening or much worse than expected?\n2) BLOOD: Check color, warmth, capillary refill and distal pulse.\n3) FEELING: Ask about numbness, tingling or burning; compare sensation.\n4) MOVE: Ask the patient to move distal fingers/toes and compare strength/movement.\n5) SWELLING: Look for increasing swelling, a tense limb, or a cast/dressing becoming tight.","id":"section-1-a042312d7a416752"},{"label":"Compartment syndrome — the emergency","text":"Think: pressure building inside the limb. Trauma/fracture → bleeding and swelling → pressure rises inside a closed muscle compartment → tissue perfusion falls → nerves and muscle can be permanently injured.","id":"section-2-a20f55d5331c88b5"},{"label":"Early warning to remember","text":"Severe or increasing pain that is out of proportion to the injury, especially pain made worse by passive stretch, is an important warning. A tense swollen compartment and new paresthesia/weakness add concern. Do not wait for every sign to appear.","id":"section-3-48a11361f51313a0"},{"label":"Critical trap","text":"A palpable distal pulse does NOT rule out compartment syndrome. Pallor, paralysis and loss of pulse are late ischemic findings; waiting for the pulse to disappear can delay treatment.","id":"section-4-9ff3b1839b5cbabd"},{"label":"Intern responsibilities","text":"Assess distal neurovascular status within your training, recognize deterioration, report findings urgently, document the change and response, and continue reassessment. Check for an obviously constrictive cast/dressing, but do not independently alter prescribed immobilization unless specifically authorized by the local protocol or order.","id":"section-5-2f3272f52ce4a521"},{"label":"Senior/ordered interventions","text":"Independent treatment decisions, modifying or removing prescribed immobilization/fixation unless specifically authorized, medication changes, compartment-pressure measurement, fasciotomy, and other invasive procedures. Your role is to recognize, report, prepare, and assist within supervision and the authorized MSF/Nasser IBD2 protocol.","id":"section-6-dfce8dba2b6fbfda"},{"label":"15-second memory","text":"Fracture patient? Check BELOW the injury: PAIN → BLOOD → FEELING → MOVE → SWELLING. Severe increasing pain + pain with passive stretch = compartment-syndrome warning. A pulse does not rule it out.","id":"section-7-10371e896c9be3cf"}]},{"itemId":"ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator","title":"External Fixator — Limb, Pins, Frame","itemType":"lesson","exams":[{"id":"ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator--standard","itemId":"ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator","itemType":"lesson","title":"External Fixator — Limb, Pins, Frame","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"stem":"Which description best matches an external fixator?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An ECG lead system around an injured limb","explanation":"This is not a fracture-fixation device."},{"id":"B","text":"Pins anchored in bone connected to a frame outside the body","explanation":"This provides the external stabilization described."},{"id":"C","text":"A compression stocking that contains no bone connection","explanation":"This is not external bone fixation."},{"id":"D","text":"A wholly internal plate with no skin-crossing pins","explanation":"This describes a different fixation approach."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"An external frame connects pins/wires anchored in bone.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator--standard--point-923ee4b215fb","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator::point-923ee4b215fb"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which clinical situation is an indication for considering external fixation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A healed fracture with no instability or correction plan","explanation":"This does not identify the acute fixation context."},{"id":"B","text":"An isolated superficial abrasion without a fracture","explanation":"This does not establish a need for bone fixation."},{"id":"C","text":"A simple soft-tissue strain without bone instability","explanation":"This does not establish a need for the described fixation system."},{"id":"D","text":"A severe open fracture with major soft-tissue injury","explanation":"External fixation may be suitable where swelling or tissue injury makes immediate internal fixation risky."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"External fixation can suit severe open/soft-tissue-compromised fractures.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator--standard--point-c36801b639ea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator::point-c36801b639ea"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which sequence provides a structured bedside review of an external fixator and the patient?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Patient name, bed number, billing, transport","explanation":"This is not a clinical fixation check."},{"id":"B","text":"Frame, stock cupboard, discharge, diet","explanation":"This is not the focused cue."},{"id":"C","text":"Limb, pins, frame, patient","explanation":"The cue begins with tissue safety before hardware and the wider patient review."},{"id":"D","text":"Pins, antibiotics, discharge, exercise","explanation":"This omits essential limb and frame assessment."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Use the limb–pins–frame–patient bedside structure.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator--standard--point-2db556f84025","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator::point-2db556f84025"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which pin-site change requires review?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Increasing redness and new drainage with fever","explanation":"These are infection warning findings."},{"id":"B","text":"An unchanged frame shape with a clean site","explanation":"No new pin-site problem is established."},{"id":"C","text":"Improving local tenderness without other changes","explanation":"This does not identify the warning pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"An unchanged clean site without new pain","explanation":"This does not describe deterioration."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"New pin-site redness/drainage/fever can indicate infection.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator--standard--point-983b27d58dc1","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator::point-983b27d58dc1"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which information determines the pin-cleaning product and schedule?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A general preference for any household antiseptic","explanation":"Product choice must be clinically authorized."},{"id":"B","text":"A product used for another patient without a matching plan","explanation":"Another patient’s care does not establish this order."},{"id":"C","text":"The authorized local order/protocol","explanation":"Pin-care products should follow the patient’s local pin-care plan."},{"id":"D","text":"The external frame’s color","explanation":"Appearance does not determine pin-care requirements."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Pin care follows aseptic technique and the authorized local plan.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator--standard--point-7681ae6f1229","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator::point-7681ae6f1229"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which pin-care plan is appropriate for a patient with an external fixator?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A preferred antiseptic chosen independently for every patient","explanation":"A universal improvised product is not the stated approach."},{"id":"B","text":"Cleaning only when drainage has become purulent","explanation":"Preventive care follows the authorized schedule, not only late changes."},{"id":"C","text":"The prior patient’s schedule without checking this order","explanation":"Different patients may have different plans."},{"id":"D","text":"Aseptic care using the ordered product and schedule","explanation":"The local authorized plan determines cleaning and dressings."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Use aseptic pin care with the authorized product and schedule.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator--standard--point-e4540c95a45d","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator::point-e4540c95a45d"],"answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":6},{"id":"ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator","itemType":"lesson","title":"External Fixator — Limb, Pins, Frame","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"The frame appears intact, but the patient reports new numbness and escalating pain. What should lead the bedside assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The last planned walking distance first","explanation":"Mobility planning follows reassessment of limb safety."},{"id":"B","text":"The distal limb and neurovascular change","explanation":"Intact-looking hardware does not establish tissue safety."},{"id":"C","text":"The routine pin-cleaning supplies first","explanation":"New neurovascular symptoms take priority."},{"id":"D","text":"The frame’s appearance as sufficient reassurance","explanation":"This would miss the clinically important change."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Assess the limb before focusing on hardware.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator--advanced--point-a37b801777da","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator::point-a37b801777da"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A frame component appears loose and movement is unexpected. Which action best addresses the immediate problem?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Wait for fever to confirm the hardware problem","explanation":"Mechanical failure need not cause fever."},{"id":"B","text":"Continue the planned unsupported mobility trial","explanation":"Unexpected movement may make this unsafe."},{"id":"C","text":"Support/protect the limb and report the hardware change","explanation":"Stabilization safety needs authorized assessment."},{"id":"D","text":"Complete the routine stock check before reporting","explanation":"A new hardware concern warrants prompt action."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Loose or broken hardware needs protection and escalation, not independent adjustment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator--advanced--point-297f9ef846ac","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator::point-297f9ef846ac"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A pin site becomes hot, swollen, and draining. What additional assessment best supports the report?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The hardware serial number without clinical findings","explanation":"A device identifier does not replace assessment."},{"id":"B","text":"The next elective clinic date without current observations","explanation":"Current deterioration needs current findings."},{"id":"C","text":"The dressing brand without a patient assessment","explanation":"Branding does not describe infection severity or limb function."},{"id":"D","text":"Vitals, pain trend, wound findings, and distal neurovascular status","explanation":"These establish local and systemic impact."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Assess the whole patient and wound, report deterioration, document, and reassess.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator--advanced--point-08afffcbe885","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-02-external-fixator::point-08afffcbe885"],"answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":3}],"bankVersion":2,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"supplied-health-alterations","filename":"05-Health-Alterations-1781195254.pdf"},{"id":"rnoh-external-fixation","title":"RNOH external fixation/bone correction and lengthening patient guide","url":"https://www.rnoh.nhs.uk/patients-and-visitors/patient-information-guides/external-fixation-bone-correction-and-lengthening"}],"sourceFields":{"sections":[{"label":"Essential meaning","text":"An external fixator stabilizes a fracture using metal pins or screws inserted into bone above and below the fracture and connected to a frame outside the body. It is often used for severe open fractures or when swelling/soft-tissue injury makes immediate internal fixation risky."},{"label":"Build the picture","text":"PINS IN BONE\\nFRAME OUTSIDE","display":"hero"},{"label":"Think first","prompt":"You walk to the bedside. What should you check first: the metal frame or the limb?","text":"The LIMB first. Reuse Lesson 1: pain → blood → feeling → move → swelling. A fixator can look intact while distal circulation or nerve function is worsening.","display":"reveal"},{"label":"Bedside sequence","text":"LIMB → PINS → FRAME → PATIENT","display":"memory"},{"label":"Scan it in two directions","text":"Visual contrast for bedside review.","display":"split","leftTitle":"LOOK FOR","leftText":"Limb: worsening pain, swelling, neurovascular change.\\nPins/wound: increasing redness, tenderness, drainage, fever.\\nFrame: loose, broken, shifted, or unexpected movement.","rightTitle":"DO NOT","rightText":"Do not independently tighten, loosen, remove, or reposition fixation hardware.\\nDo not invent a pin-cleaning product or schedule; follow the authorized IBD2 order/protocol."},{"label":"LOOSE","text":"Loose/broken/shifted hardware is a protection-and-escalation problem. Support the limb, limit unsafe movement, report it, and do not adjust the fixation system yourself.","display":"stop"},{"label":"Pin / wound care","text":"Pins that enter bone create infection risk. Use strict aseptic technique for pin care, clean/dress the sites according to the authorized health-care/local protocol, and document abnormal findings. Report meaningful changes promptly."},{"label":"Quick decision","prompt":"A pin site is more painful, red and swollen with new drainage. What is your nursing move?","text":"Assess the patient and wound, check temperature/vitals and distal neurovascular status, report the deterioration, document it, and continue reassessment. Follow the local IBD2 pin/wound-care and antimicrobial plan rather than choosing a treatment yourself.","display":"reveal"},{"label":"10-second memory","text":"LIMB → PINS → FRAME → PATIENT. Neurovascular change, infection signs, or a hardware problem = assess, protect, escalate, document, reassess.","display":"memory"}]},"readingSections":[{"label":"Essential meaning","text":"An external fixator stabilizes a fracture using metal pins or screws inserted into bone above and below the fracture and connected to a frame outside the body. It is often used for severe open fractures or when swelling/soft-tissue injury makes immediate internal fixation risky.","id":"section-0-d679bdefc5bed92b"},{"label":"Build the picture","text":"PINS IN BONE\\nFRAME OUTSIDE","id":"section-1-889c7721ca32ee2b"},{"label":"Think first","text":"You walk to the bedside. What should you check first: the metal frame or the limb?\n\nThe LIMB first. Reuse Lesson 1: pain → blood → feeling → move → swelling. A fixator can look intact while distal circulation or nerve function is worsening.","id":"section-2-1296a9db59f636fc"},{"label":"Bedside sequence","text":"LIMB → PINS → FRAME → PATIENT","id":"section-3-86d00a5a53f9149f"},{"label":"Scan it in two directions","text":"Visual contrast for bedside review.\n\nLOOK FOR\n\nLimb: worsening pain, swelling, neurovascular change.\\nPins/wound: increasing redness, tenderness, drainage, fever.\\nFrame: loose, broken, shifted, or unexpected movement.\n\nDO NOT\n\nDo not independently tighten, loosen, remove, or reposition fixation hardware.\\nDo not invent a pin-cleaning product or schedule; follow the authorized IBD2 order/protocol.","id":"section-4-59130a58e7d35a3a"},{"label":"LOOSE","text":"Loose/broken/shifted hardware is a protection-and-escalation problem. Support the limb, limit unsafe movement, report it, and do not adjust the fixation system yourself.","id":"section-5-ebf2136cd38edd54"},{"label":"Pin / wound care","text":"Pins that enter bone create infection risk. Use strict aseptic technique for pin care, clean/dress the sites according to the authorized health-care/local protocol, and document abnormal findings. Report meaningful changes promptly.","id":"section-6-56e3c79b9220e80a"},{"label":"Quick decision","text":"A pin site is more painful, red and swollen with new drainage. What is your nursing move?\n\nAssess the patient and wound, check temperature/vitals and distal neurovascular status, report the deterioration, document it, and continue reassessment. Follow the local IBD2 pin/wound-care and antimicrobial plan rather than choosing a treatment yourself.","id":"section-7-9deca8119d53b79d"},{"label":"10-second memory","text":"LIMB → PINS → FRAME → PATIENT. Neurovascular change, infection signs, or a hardware problem = assess, protect, escalate, document, reassess.","id":"section-8-3ba70f1233025515"}]},{"itemId":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame","title":"Taylor Spatial Frame (TSF) — Bedside Nursing","itemType":"lesson","exams":[{"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--standard","itemId":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame","itemType":"lesson","title":"Taylor Spatial Frame (TSF) — Bedside Nursing","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"stem":"What does Taylor Spatial Frame describe?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A circular hexapod external fixation system","explanation":"It stabilizes bone and allows planned multidirectional correction."},{"id":"B","text":"A wholly internal intramedullary nail","explanation":"That is a different fixation method."},{"id":"C","text":"A passive compression bandage","explanation":"That is not a hexapod fixation frame."},{"id":"D","text":"A cardiac transcutaneous pacing system","explanation":"This is not an orthopaedic TSF."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"TSF is a hexapod circular external fixator for stabilization and correction.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--standard--point-b006317b7879","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-b006317b7879"],"answerKey":"A"},{"point":"The principal hexapod arrangement has six adjustable struts.","format":"numeric-entry","stem":"How many adjustable telescopic struts connect the principal rings in the basic TSF arrangement?","answer":6,"unit":"struts","tolerance":0,"explanation":"The hexapod arrangement uses six main struts; complex frames may add components.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--standard--point-cbb3ccedabc0","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-cbb3ccedabc0"]},{"stem":"Which TSF components pass through skin into bone to connect the limb to the frame?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Wires and/or half-pins","explanation":"They provide the bone-to-frame attachment."},{"id":"B","text":"Telescopic strut bodies","explanation":"Struts connect the external rings rather than serving as the skin-crossing bone anchors."},{"id":"C","text":"The ring circumference alone","explanation":"Rings surround the limb; pins/wires provide attachment."},{"id":"D","text":"The software-generated paper schedule","explanation":"Instructions are not a physical bone attachment."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Pins/wires connect bone to the external rings.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--standard--point-e57f651c3d2f","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-e57f651c3d2f"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"What is a purpose of a Taylor Spatial Frame?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Treatment of every fever without an infection assessment","explanation":"A frame is not an antimicrobial treatment."},{"id":"B","text":"Correction of an ECG conduction delay","explanation":"This is an orthopaedic rather than cardiac device."},{"id":"C","text":"Immediate correction of every postoperative pain episode","explanation":"Pain requires assessment rather than automatic frame adjustment."},{"id":"D","text":"Planned deformity correction or limb lengthening","explanation":"The system can support gradual individualized bone correction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"TSF may be used for complex fractures, deformity, lengthening, or reconstruction.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--standard--point-651505cdaed0","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-651505cdaed0"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"What determines planned TSF strut-length changes?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The intern’s preferred direction of visible alignment","explanation":"Visual preference does not authorize a correction."},{"id":"B","text":"The patient’s pain score as a direct turn-count formula","explanation":"Pain is not an independent adjustment prescription."},{"id":"C","text":"An individualized written correction schedule based on the surgeon’s plan","explanation":"Measurements, imaging, and software support the authorized schedule."},{"id":"D","text":"A universal daily turn count used for every TSF","explanation":"The plan is individualized rather than universal."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Correction follows a surgeon-planned individualized schedule.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--standard--point-d1501cbdc26f","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-d1501cbdc26f"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which set best checks limb safety before inspecting the rest of a TSF?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The next scheduled X-ray date without current findings","explanation":"A future study does not replace bedside assessment."},{"id":"B","text":"Pain trend, perfusion, sensation, movement, swelling, and pressure points","explanation":"These assess tissue and nerve function distal to the injury."},{"id":"C","text":"Ring count and paint condition without a limb examination","explanation":"Device appearance alone cannot establish tissue safety."},{"id":"D","text":"Strut length without a distal examination","explanation":"A length reading does not assess nerve or blood supply."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Distal neurovascular assessment precedes frame-focused reassurance.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--standard--point-0cfa2609d327","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-0cfa2609d327"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which TSF change needs prompt reporting?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A stable written schedule without new symptoms","explanation":"This does not establish deterioration."},{"id":"B","text":"An unchanged pin site with improving comfort","explanation":"This is not the warning pattern."},{"id":"C","text":"A stable alignment without skin pressure or symptoms","explanation":"No new problem is described."},{"id":"D","text":"New purulent drainage or unusual frame movement","explanation":"Infection and hardware changes are both explicit red flags."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize pin infection, hardware failure, skin pressure, and alignment red flags.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--standard--point-1445ea942bfc","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-1445ea942bfc"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which note best supports reassessment after a TSF concern?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"“Frame checked; fine” without findings or timing","explanation":"This does not document the actual assessment or response."},{"id":"B","text":"“Doctor aware” without the change or received plan","explanation":"The note lacks the information needed for follow-up."},{"id":"C","text":"Timed findings, change from baseline, person notified, instructions, and response","explanation":"These describe the event and follow-up objectively."},{"id":"D","text":"“Pain expected” without the trend or limb examination","explanation":"This can obscure a clinically important change."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Document time, change, baseline comparison, notification, instructions, and response.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--standard--point-4bb6b362ceb3","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-4bb6b362ceb3"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":8},{"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame","itemType":"lesson","title":"Taylor Spatial Frame (TSF) — Bedside Nursing","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A trained patient adjusts their own TSF under a written schedule. An intern is asked to change the schedule because the limb looks different. Which distinction matters?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A patient request automatically substitutes for a surgical order","explanation":"A request does not authorize a plan change."},{"id":"B","text":"All TSF adjustments are prohibited even under a patient’s own plan","explanation":"A patient may perform this self-care after individual training and authorization."},{"id":"C","text":"Authorized individual self-care versus a new unsupervised adjustment decision","explanation":"Patient training does not authorize an intern to alter the surgical plan."},{"id":"D","text":"A visible alignment difference provides a numerical correction by itself","explanation":"It does not replace the planned assessment and schedule."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Trained patient self-adjustment does not authorize an intern to improvise.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--advanced--point-d1b91e4aefdd","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-d1b91e4aefdd"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A TSF patient has rapidly worsening pain, passive-stretch pain, and new paresthesia while pulses remain present. Which complication is the urgent concern?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Acute compartment syndrome","explanation":"Pulse presence does not exclude threatened compartment perfusion."},{"id":"B","text":"Expected adjustment discomfort established by the pulse","explanation":"The warning cluster needs emergency assessment."},{"id":"C","text":"An isolated cosmetic frame concern","explanation":"New pain and sensory change are tissue-safety findings."},{"id":"D","text":"A normal recovery established by intact hardware","explanation":"Hardware appearance does not establish neurovascular safety."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Pulses can persist during early compartment syndrome.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--advanced--point-71d0565ec147","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-71d0565ec147"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A TSF patient wants to increase weight bearing, but the current order is unclear. Which information should determine the activity plan?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The number of struts as a universal loading rule","explanation":"Strut count does not determine the individual order."},{"id":"B","text":"The patient’s ability to tolerate one unsupported step","explanation":"Tolerance does not replace the structural treatment plan."},{"id":"C","text":"Another TSF patient’s walking allowance","explanation":"Different reconstructions can have different restrictions."},{"id":"D","text":"The verified patient-specific weight-bearing order","explanation":"Device presence alone does not establish permitted loading."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Weight bearing and wound/pin care follow prescribed orders.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--advanced--point-bb61ba030fb4","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-bb61ba030fb4"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A TSF patient’s new sensory change was reported and a management instruction received. Which subsequent action closes the bedside response?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Repeat relevant neurovascular observations and document the response","explanation":"Reporting does not replace ongoing assessment of tissue safety."},{"id":"B","text":"Document only the hardware appearance","explanation":"The patient’s neurovascular response remains central."},{"id":"C","text":"Assume notification establishes resolution","explanation":"Notification is not a measured clinical outcome."},{"id":"D","text":"Return to routine checks without comparing the change","explanation":"A new problem requires follow-up of the affected findings."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Reassess after a clinical change or intervention.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--advanced--point-573a71c13deb","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-573a71c13deb"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Distal pulses remain present, but a TSF contact point is causing new skin breakdown. Which interpretation fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Skin breakdown authorizes independent frame adjustment","explanation":"The intern must not manipulate hardware independently."},{"id":"B","text":"A pressure injury still needs assessment and reporting","explanation":"Preserved pulses do not make a local skin injury harmless."},{"id":"C","text":"The absence of fever establishes that no action is needed","explanation":"Pressure injury need not be accompanied by fever."},{"id":"D","text":"Normal pulses establish that the contact pressure is acceptable","explanation":"Neurovascular and skin assessments address different risks."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Assess and document skin pressure points near the frame.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame--advanced--point-74f0afc57bfb","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-quick-taylor-spatial-frame::point-74f0afc57bfb"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":5}],"bankVersion":2,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"supplied-health-alterations","filename":"05-Health-Alterations-1781195254.pdf"},{"id":"rnoh-external-fixation","title":"RNOH external fixation/bone correction and lengthening patient guide","url":"https://www.rnoh.nhs.uk/patients-and-visitors/patient-information-guides/external-fixation-bone-correction-and-lengthening"}],"sourceFields":{"sections":[{"label":"Essential meaning","text":"TSF = Taylor Spatial Frame. It is a hexapod circular external fixator used to hold and gradually correct the position of bones. Unlike a simple bar fixator, it can make precise changes in multiple directions."},{"label":"Recognize the parts","text":"Metal rings surround the limb. Wires and/or half-pins pass through the skin into bone, connecting bone to the frame. Six adjustable telescopic struts connect the main rings; complex frames may have additional rings/struts."},{"label":"Why it is used","text":"Selected complex fractures; deformity correction; limb lengthening; difficult or poorly healed fractures (nonunion/malunion), and some limb reconstruction cases. The reason for this patient's TSF depends on the surgeon's plan."},{"label":"How correction works","text":"A surgeon plans the correction using measurements, X-rays, and software. Strut lengths may be changed according to an individualized written schedule. Trained patients/caregivers sometimes make their OWN scheduled adjustments after instruction; an intern must never initiate, change, or improvise a strut adjustment."},{"label":"Assessment: LIMB first","text":"Start distal to the injured limb: pain and trend, colour/warmth, capillary refill, pulses, sensation, toe/finger movement, swelling and skin pressure points. Compare sides when possible. Then assess pin sites, frame integrity, vital signs and overall comfort."},{"label":"Red flag — acute compartment syndrome","text":"Severe or rapidly increasing pain out of proportion (especially on passive stretch), tense swelling, new tingling/numbness or weakness = urgent suspicion. Pulses can remain present early; do not wait for pallor or absent pulse. Escalate immediately for emergency orthopaedic assessment."},{"label":"Pin-site and frame red flags","text":"Report increasing pin-site pain, spreading redness, swelling, new/purulent discharge, fever or feeling unwell. Also report loose/broken wires or struts, unusual frame movement, skin pressure injury or a sudden change in alignment. Do not manipulate hardware yourself."},{"label":"First nursing action & escalation","text":"If abnormal: check patient/vitals and repeat focused neurovascular assessment; protect/support the limb and stop unsafe movement; inform the responsible nurse and orthopaedic team promptly. For suspected compartment syndrome or marked loss of perfusion/sensation/movement, escalate as an emergency. Follow the prescribed wound/pin-care and weight-bearing orders; assist only within supervised intern scope."},{"label":"Reassessment & documentation","text":"Repeat pain and neurovascular observations at the ordered frequency and after a clinical change/intervention. Record time, findings, comparison to baseline, person notified, instructions received and patient response. Recheck pin sites, skin contact points and frame stability visually."},{"label":"Common mistakes","text":"Focusing on the frame instead of the limb; treating worsening pain as 'normal TSF pain'; assuming palpable pulses exclude compartment syndrome; independently turning struts or tightening pins; choosing a pin-cleaning solution or allowing weight-bearing without the local plan."},{"label":"10-second memory","text":"TSF = RINGS + PINS/WIRES + SIX STRUTS. At bedside: LIMB → PINS → FRAME → PATIENT. New neurovascular problem, infection signs, or broken/loose hardware = assess, protect, escalate, document, reassess.","display":"memory"}]},"readingSections":[{"label":"Essential meaning","text":"TSF = Taylor Spatial Frame. It is a hexapod circular external fixator used to hold and gradually correct the position of bones. Unlike a simple bar fixator, it can make precise changes in multiple directions.","id":"section-0-690588ae6e89b2c0"},{"label":"Recognize the parts","text":"Metal rings surround the limb. Wires and/or half-pins pass through the skin into bone, connecting bone to the frame. Six adjustable telescopic struts connect the main rings; complex frames may have additional rings/struts.","id":"section-1-eaca562e9672d6a5"},{"label":"Why it is used","text":"Selected complex fractures; deformity correction; limb lengthening; difficult or poorly healed fractures (nonunion/malunion), and some limb reconstruction cases. The reason for this patient's TSF depends on the surgeon's plan.","id":"section-2-e039187504b4d800"},{"label":"How correction works","text":"A surgeon plans the correction using measurements, X-rays, and software. Strut lengths may be changed according to an individualized written schedule. Trained patients/caregivers sometimes make their OWN scheduled adjustments after instruction; an intern must never initiate, change, or improvise a strut adjustment.","id":"section-3-8ad9980286682f72"},{"label":"Assessment: LIMB first","text":"Start distal to the injured limb: pain and trend, colour/warmth, capillary refill, pulses, sensation, toe/finger movement, swelling and skin pressure points. Compare sides when possible. Then assess pin sites, frame integrity, vital signs and overall comfort.","id":"section-4-3d4f99ec14d14053"},{"label":"Red flag — acute compartment syndrome","text":"Severe or rapidly increasing pain out of proportion (especially on passive stretch), tense swelling, new tingling/numbness or weakness = urgent suspicion. Pulses can remain present early; do not wait for pallor or absent pulse. Escalate immediately for emergency orthopaedic assessment.","id":"section-5-cdd3e613dfd77ccf"},{"label":"Pin-site and frame red flags","text":"Report increasing pin-site pain, spreading redness, swelling, new/purulent discharge, fever or feeling unwell. Also report loose/broken wires or struts, unusual frame movement, skin pressure injury or a sudden change in alignment. Do not manipulate hardware yourself.","id":"section-6-2ee59c59c4a42289"},{"label":"First nursing action & escalation","text":"If abnormal: check patient/vitals and repeat focused neurovascular assessment; protect/support the limb and stop unsafe movement; inform the responsible nurse and orthopaedic team promptly. For suspected compartment syndrome or marked loss of perfusion/sensation/movement, escalate as an emergency. Follow the prescribed wound/pin-care and weight-bearing orders; assist only within supervised intern scope.","id":"section-7-d01bbf9c2cd12be0"},{"label":"Reassessment & documentation","text":"Repeat pain and neurovascular observations at the ordered frequency and after a clinical change/intervention. Record time, findings, comparison to baseline, person notified, instructions received and patient response. Recheck pin sites, skin contact points and frame stability visually.","id":"section-8-bf1b53b681cf7298"},{"label":"Common mistakes","text":"Focusing on the frame instead of the limb; treating worsening pain as 'normal TSF pain'; assuming palpable pulses exclude compartment syndrome; independently turning struts or tightening pins; choosing a pin-cleaning solution or allowing weight-bearing without the local plan.","id":"section-9-7a44e591801c34e8"},{"label":"10-second memory","text":"TSF = RINGS + PINS/WIRES + SIX STRUTS. At bedside: LIMB → PINS → FRAME → PATIENT. New neurovascular problem, infection signs, or broken/loose hardware = assess, protect, escalate, document, reassess.","id":"section-10-0fa1b825b8464dac"}]},{"itemId":"ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis","title":"Osteomyelitis — Think Deeper Infection","itemType":"lesson","exams":[{"id":"ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis--standard","itemId":"ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis","itemType":"lesson","title":"Osteomyelitis — Think Deeper Infection","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"stem":"Which description correctly defines osteomyelitis?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Infection of bone","explanation":"It may follow blood spread, nearby infection, or direct entry after trauma."},{"id":"B","text":"An isolated tendon rupture","explanation":"This affects a different structure."},{"id":"C","text":"An isolated reduction in bone density","explanation":"This is not the definition of infection."},{"id":"D","text":"A closed compartment-pressure syndrome alone","explanation":"This is a different mechanism."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Osteomyelitis is bone infection with possible blood, contiguous, or direct entry.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis--standard--point-9634b709b2ee","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis::point-9634b709b2ee"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which cluster warrants consideration of deeper bone infection?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A normal distal examination without wound symptoms","explanation":"This does not establish bone infection."},{"id":"B","text":"A stable old scar without pain or drainage","explanation":"No active warning finding is described."},{"id":"C","text":"A healed wound with steadily improving comfort","explanation":"This does not identify ongoing deeper infection."},{"id":"D","text":"Persistent deep pain with drainage and poor healing","explanation":"These findings raise concern for osteomyelitis."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize persistent deep pain, drainage, poor healing, and inflammation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis--standard--point-168c0b764886","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis::point-168c0b764886"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which set best supports a nursing report of suspected osteomyelitis deterioration?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A tablet count without symptoms or observations","explanation":"Supply does not establish the clinical condition."},{"id":"B","text":"A device photograph without a patient assessment","explanation":"Appearance alone is incomplete."},{"id":"C","text":"Vitals, wound drainage, pain trend, and distal findings","explanation":"These establish local, systemic, and limb-safety information."},{"id":"D","text":"A dressing brand and the original appointment date","explanation":"These do not describe current severity."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Assess vitals, wound/drainage, pain trend, and neurovascular status.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis--standard--point-7088ef6f0968","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis::point-7088ef6f0968"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which treatment pattern is compatible with osteomyelitis?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A vitamin supplement as complete infection treatment","explanation":"Nutritional support does not replace antimicrobial/surgical care."},{"id":"B","text":"A single analgesic dose as complete infection treatment","explanation":"Pain relief does not eradicate bone infection."},{"id":"C","text":"A routine bronchodilator as complete infection treatment","explanation":"This does not treat a bone infection."},{"id":"D","text":"An extended antimicrobial plan with possible debridement","explanation":"Both medical and surgical treatment may be required."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Treatment may include prolonged antibiotics and surgery/debridement.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis--standard--point-0216476272b3","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis::point-0216476272b3"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which routes can lead to osteomyelitis?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Nearby infection only, without bloodstream or traumatic entry","explanation":"This excludes two routes."},{"id":"B","text":"Bloodstream spread, nearby infection, direct traumatic entry","explanation":"These are the three routes."},{"id":"C","text":"Bloodstream spread only, without local or traumatic entry","explanation":"This excludes two routes."},{"id":"D","text":"Direct traumatic entry only, without bloodstream spread","explanation":"This excludes hematogenous and nearby spread."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Bone infection may arise through bloodstream, contiguous spread, or direct inoculation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis--standard--point-51503b971fef","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis::point-51503b971fef"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":5},{"id":"ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis","itemType":"lesson","title":"Osteomyelitis — Think Deeper Infection","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"A patient says a persistently draining wound is normal because it has drained for weeks. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Chronicity does not make persistent drainage benign","explanation":"Deeper infection and poor healing still need assessment."},{"id":"B","text":"Chronic drainage establishes eradication of infection","explanation":"Ongoing drainage does not establish cure."},{"id":"C","text":"Absence of a new injury proves the drainage harmless","explanation":"Ongoing infection does not require a new injury."},{"id":"D","text":"A long duration excludes bone involvement","explanation":"Persistent wounds and bone pain can support concern for deeper infection."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Chronic drainage is not automatically normal.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis--advanced--point-d3bdd3b2690b","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis::point-d3bdd3b2690b"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A patient with a draining orthopaedic wound becomes febrile, hypotensive, and confused. Which response has priority?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Complete the next planned education session first","explanation":"This delays assessment of acute systemic compromise."},{"id":"B","text":"Treat drainage as the expected baseline without reassessment","explanation":"The systemic change invalidates that reassurance."},{"id":"C","text":"Wait for the next elective wound review","explanation":"New instability requires earlier action."},{"id":"D","text":"Assess ABCs/current observations and urgently escalate systemic deterioration","explanation":"The presentation may reflect a serious infection complication."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Escalate systemic deterioration or worsening infection/hardware problems.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis--advanced--point-a91227805c79","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-lesson-04-osteomyelitis::point-a91227805c79"],"answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":2}],"bankVersion":2,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"supplied-health-alterations","filename":"05-Health-Alterations-1781195254.pdf"}],"sourceFields":{"sections":[{"label":"Meaning","text":"Osteomyelitis = infection of bone. It may spread from blood, nearby tissue, or enter directly after trauma/open injury."},{"label":"Think of it when","text":"Persistent deep pain, drainage, poor healing, redness/swelling ± fever."},{"label":"Nursing check","text":"Vitals → wound/drainage → pain trend → neurovascular status."},{"label":"Escalate","text":"Worsening infection, systemic deterioration, or hardware/fixator problem."},{"label":"Treatment","text":"May include prolonged antibiotics plus surgery/debridement."},{"label":"Common mistake","text":"Assuming chronic drainage is “normal”."},{"label":"Memory","text":"Persistent wound + persistent bone pain → think deeper infection.","display":"memory"}]},"readingSections":[{"label":"Meaning","text":"Osteomyelitis = infection of bone. It may spread from blood, nearby tissue, or enter directly after trauma/open injury.","id":"section-0-ef8b4af42aeb7edb"},{"label":"Think of it when","text":"Persistent deep pain, drainage, poor healing, redness/swelling ± fever.","id":"section-1-65f299e067332ad0"},{"label":"Nursing check","text":"Vitals → wound/drainage → pain trend → neurovascular status.","id":"section-2-6b317abd31338f0d"},{"label":"Escalate","text":"Worsening infection, systemic deterioration, or hardware/fixator problem.","id":"section-3-cc30e652d3114130"},{"label":"Treatment","text":"May include prolonged antibiotics plus surgery/debridement.","id":"section-4-58a3d766599e5690"},{"label":"Common mistake","text":"Assuming chronic drainage is “normal”.","id":"section-5-e4ac905d8dbe80b7"},{"label":"Memory","text":"Persistent wound + persistent bone pain → think deeper infection.","id":"section-6-a84d6a1c14d03e46"}]}]);
